[Music]  Condoleezza Rice served on President  George Herbert Walker Bush 'iz National  Security Council  she was his senior director of Soviet  and East European affairs and special  assistant to the president for national  security affairs later on rice was  Secretary of State and national security  adviser under his son president george w  bush she's with us now for an interview  you'll only see you'll see first on CBS  this morning Secretary Rice good morning  one of the measures of leadership is  that leaders make the people who work  for them better how did George - what  did you learn from George Herbert well I  learned from President Bush how to be a  good public servant and what that meant  I learned to care about relationships  before you need it to call it somebody I  remember as a young staffer I got this  note early in February said I want to  congratulate Helmut Kohl on his victory  in the Bundestag and I was the  specialist I thought what victory an  accompanist on but he was reaching out  before he had to ask Helmut Kohl to do  something difficult for German  unification and that's who he was he  absolutely valued relationships and and  valued people and I I think I learned a  lot from him about that I don't love so  much about how he loved his family I saw  an interview that he did with Jena his  granddaughter where he said I used to  fear death but I don't anymore because I  think that afterlife is going to be  pretty good but he really loved being  with his family can you tell his family  story Krystal there were people of great  faith and he was a man of great faith  and so I'm sure he was actually looking  forward to seeing mrs. barbara again  started Robin who he grieved for  throughout his life but I remember being  up at Kennebunkport for a church service  with the blen we were sitting outside  and and the priest was a little bit kind  of new age as somebody who was visiting  and he said now I mean nobody it does  anybody out they have a perfect family  and it was of course meant ironically  and George hatred if you put raised his  head and said I do of course he knew  that they were not a perfect family but  it was to me a statement of his  unconditional love for his children for  his entire family and I think one of the  reasons that I bonded with them was  because I had that sense of family  he came to California and after he I had  left government he met my father it was  one of the great moments for my dad and  so family it's just so important to the  bushes and I love that President Bush  and his national security adviser Brent  Scowcroft credited you with helping them  write their book about the end of the  Cold War how did you help President Bush  reflect on that era only one term  president but one of the most  significant periods in American history  well just to help remind them how really  significant it was because in fact these  are people who it was never about them  when that's famous story of after the  Berlin Wall fell all of us rushing to  the Oval Office and saying mr. president  you have to go to Berlin for Truman and  for Kennedy and for Reagan and he said  what would I do dance on the wall and  then he said something else he said this  is a German moment not an American  moment and I note that notice that  Angela Merkel the German Chancellor is  here he really was in so many ways the  father of German unification because he  had to bolster Helmut Kohl he had to  bring Margaret Thatcher and meet Iran  along who were somewhat nervous about  the unification of Germany but it was  never about him and I think that's what  made him such a great president at a  time when the Soviet Union had to be  quietly put to rest to be triumphalist  at that moment would have been very  dangerous it would never have occurred  to him to be triumphalist that was self  equality and what would make him cranky  I'm Kerry how many wonderful stories  about him well humility was his best  quality  I also remember telling him that  Gorbachev do you remember mr. president  the Gorbachev made his last call to you  before the Soviet Union went out of  existence he made it to you and he  thought nothing remarkable about that  so that's he was what made him cranky  being late or anybody else it was late  and he shares that with his son did you  ever play speed golf or the meet you  I did play speed golf with him up at  Cape Arundel golf course and I mean guys  it means that you you barely hit your  shot before you're moving on to the next  one and you really don't let anybody  else hit their shot before they're  you're moving on I can remember putting  with him you know reading a putt what  was that you just kind of put it and  moved on he never wasted a moment even  on the golf course right it's almost  like polo out of a golf cart but you do  actually get out of the cart  returning back to his presidency though  you talk about humility what would he  say now about I mean obviously he's  being eulogized and remembered but I  wouldn't he also want people to know  about the reality of some of the  decisions you make and the toughness of  it and it's not all roses and perfect  decisions I think that's such an  important point because as we look back  we see a road that worked out so well  Germany unified Eastern Europe was  liberated the Soviet Union had a  peaceful death that was thousands of  little decisions along the way some of  them incredibly consequential some of  them very tough do you tell the Germans  you know you really do have to stay in  NATO as unified Germany can you find a  way to deal with the Soviet Union that  is collapsing in a way that's with  dignity but without giving up on the  principles that the United States had  held a since the end of World War Two  and these decisions in retrospect look  easy in fact they were hard and they  were tough but George HW Bush was always  out ahead of us in some ways because he  had principles that let him we didn't  spend much time thinking should we unify  Germany because he had great faith in a  democratic Germany that had for more  than 45 years been a good ally of the  United States and so that is the the  lesson of this that great public  servants do their jobs in some ways so  well that they achieve the goals and  when we look back we don't even realize  how hard it was it's been fun to look  back at his life Connie and I think if  you live to 94 for the most part healthy  and at the end you die with the people  that love you love and love you  including your dog that's a really  beautiful way to go I think absolutely  it is  and I think that this was a life of his  was life of consequence that's quite  clear in his presidency you know even  domestically the Americans with  Disabilities Act how many Americans  today can go to a football game or to a  restaurant because of that a life of  consequence but also a life surrounded  by family and friends one of the things  that I admire about the bushes in  general but him in particular friends  from decades and decades and decades ago  when your friends stay with you no  matter how famous or how much how much  difficulty you get into you know that  you've lived a great life your race has  been run as as the Bible would say  you've run your race well yeah there's a  story about the caretaker who took care  of the place at Kennebunkport who was  invited today and was very heartened to  be included it's it's it's amazing and  you know there will be people there  Kennebunkport there will be there people  there from from Texas and there will be  world leaders there Thank You secretary  Conte

